WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.040
The top sports stars get there
with a lot of coaching -

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.000
not just physical
but also psychological.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.080
I work with sports people
all the time,

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.160
and here are a few secrets from
the world of sports psychology.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:15.200
And it turns out, these tricks are
equally relevant in everyday life.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.920
So next time you feel
really nervous, try this.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.040
It might sound a bit strange,
but I promise you it works.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.520
Tell yourself:
“I am not nervous, I am excited!”

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:31.360
Why does this work?

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.360
Well, we all get anxious
and nervous at times -

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.600
totally normal.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.240
The tendency of course
is to tell yourself to calm down. 

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.320
Sounds like a good idea,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:43.960
but having butterflies
in your stomach,

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.120
and that sense of anxiety,
is just so far removed

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.840
from a state of calm,
your body just won't buy it.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.280
Reframing anxiety as excitement

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.920
works because both
are states of high arousal.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.520
But while anxiety is negative
and will hamper your performance -

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.640
you literally feel under threat -

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.680
excitement is an emotion
that will help you feel positive

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:05.560
and look forward to the challenge.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.400
In the words of Britain's most
decorated Olympian -

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.560
with six gold medals -
track cyclist Sir Chris Hoy: 

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.240
All athletes learn
how to take care of themselves.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.040
But as important as
taking care of yourself

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.480
is learning to let others
support you along the way.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.520
In 2019, Kenyan long-distance
runner Eliud Kipchoge

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.040
broke the two-hour marathon record. 
But, he didn't do it on his own.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.440
On the day of the event,
he had help from 41 pacemakers,

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:43.560
and of course the support

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:45.920
of thousands of people
who came along to cheer. 

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.720
Kipchoge asked for
the general public to be there

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.600
because he knew he could
run better with their support. 

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.560
Who can you ask to support you
in your everyday life? 

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.960
There is no shame in getting help

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.960
when you're trying to achieve
something difficult.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.960
Asking for support
is a sign of strength,

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.720
and positive support is priceless.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.400
Many of the world's top athletes

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:13.320
use visualisation techniques
before a big event -

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.680
they're mentally rehearsing
it in their minds.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:19.560
This can increase motivation,
build confidence

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:21.280
and improve your performance.  

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.800
The more realistic this visualisation
and preparation is,

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:26.680
the more successful it will be.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:31.400
Athletes will think about the sounds
they might hear - like the crowd.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:34.760
What they might smell -
like freshly cut grass.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.520
And imagine what
they are going to see.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.240
And how they might feel.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:42.280
Runners might visualise
difficult parts of their race.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:46.120
Before becoming the fastest
British woman in recorded history,

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.880
Dina Asher-Smith had already raced -
and won - multiple times in her mind.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:52.320
She said:

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.800
So next time you have something
to prepare for -

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.920
like a job interview or an exam -

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.200
visualise exactly how you
would like it to go.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.640
Think about what you expect to see,

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:15.200
to smell, to hear
and how it will make you feel. 

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.240
Identify any mental challenges
and how you could overcome these.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:23.040
So that when you come to do it
for real, you are fully prepared.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.160
Negative thoughts reduce confidence.

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:31.560
This happens to everyone -
even the greatest athletes. 

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.480
But they have learned
techniques to overcome them.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.320
One technique is called self-talk -
literally talking to yourself. 

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:41.280
This can have a surprising effect
on your performance.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.000
Top athletes will often
be heard saying,

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:45.120
“You got this."

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:46.400
"I can do this.” 

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:49.200
It helps them to control anxiety,

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.840
to build their confidence
and to provide focus.

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:56.720
Muhammad Ali, often considered
the greatest boxer of all time,

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:58.200
was famous for saying: 

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:04.000
But interestingly, he admitted
repeatedly saying this phrase

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.720
to himself long before
he became successful.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.040
You don't need to say much.
Use small phrases like:

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:12.040
“You are ready.”
“You are good at this.”

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:16.720
So, next time your brain tells you
you can't do something,

